= Intersubjectivity — Simple =

'''Intersubjectivity''' is the relationship between different subjects or perspectives that makes shared meaning, shared reference, and coordinated understanding possible.

Two people can look at the same object from different positions, talk about it, correct misunderstandings, and still recognize that they are referring to the same thing.

They do not need to have identical experiences.

== Different perspectives can still connect ==

Suppose two people stand on opposite sides of a table.

They see different surfaces, but they can still understand that they are looking at the same table.

In phenomenology, philosophers such as Edmund Husserl studied how we experience other people as subjects with their own perspectives and how a shared world becomes possible.<ref name="husserl">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/husserl/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Edmund Husserl.”]</ref>

This is a philosophical problem, not simply a test of whether two people agree.

== Agreement is not enough ==

Two people can agree because they copied the same mistake.

They can also understand each other very well while disagreeing.

So:

<pre>
agreement
does not automatically mean
shared understanding or truth
</pre>

Intersubjectivity concerns the relation between perspectives, not just matching answers.

== Babies and caregivers ==

Developmental psychologists study early forms of intersubjectivity.

'''Primary intersubjectivity''' refers to early face-to-face coordination between an infant and caregiver.

Examples include:

* mutual attention;
* vocal turn-taking;
* facial interaction;
* emotional coordination.

These behaviors show reciprocal interaction.

They do not give researchers direct access to what the infant subjectively experiences.

== Joint attention ==

Later in infancy, children increasingly coordinate attention with another person toward an object.

This is sometimes called '''secondary intersubjectivity'''.

A child may:

* follow another person's gaze;
* point to an object;
* look back at the caregiver;
* share attention to the same event.

This creates a three-part relation:

<pre>
child ↔ caregiver
   ↘       ↙
     object
</pre>

Joint attention is important for language and social learning.<ref name="terrace">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9116197/ Terrace, Bigelow and Beebe (2022), “Intersubjectivity and the Emergence of Words.”]</ref>

== Looking at the same thing is not always joint attention ==

Two people might happen to look at the same object without knowing the other person is looking too.

Joint attention usually requires some coordination between:

* self;
* other person;
* shared object.

Researchers therefore look for behaviors such as gaze checking, pointing, or coordinated response.

== Common ground ==

[[Common ground]] is information people treat as mutually shared.

A conversation becomes easier when both people know:

* what has already been discussed;
* what object is being referred to;
* what words mean in the current situation.

Common ground can be incomplete.

People often discover missing common ground only when a misunderstanding happens.

== Grounding and repair ==

People maintain understanding through '''grounding'''.

They may say:

* “yes”;
* “the red one?”;
* “do you mean the second door?”;
* “no, I meant the other one.”

These small repairs are important.

Intersubjective understanding is often created through correction rather than perfect understanding from the beginning.

== Theory of mind ==

[[Theory of mind]] is about understanding or attributing mental states such as beliefs and intentions to other people.

Intersubjectivity is broader.

People can coordinate attention and action without explicitly reasoning about every mental state.

The two concepts overlap but are not identical.

== Group cognition ==

[[Group cognition]] studies how groups think and solve problems through interaction.

Intersubjectivity helps make this possible because participants need enough shared reference and mutual understanding to coordinate.

But group cognition also includes division of labor, shared tools, and distributed memory.

== Studying intersubjectivity ==

Researchers can use:

* joint-attention experiments;
* gaze tracking;
* conversation analysis;
* referential communication tasks;
* interviews;
* observation.

A communication experiment might ask one person to describe an unfamiliar shape so another person can identify it.

Over repeated trials, the pair may develop a shared nickname.

Researchers can measure accuracy and how quickly the description becomes shorter.

== Synchrony is not proof ==

People sometimes synchronize their movement, speech, or physiology during interaction.

Synchrony can show coupling.

It does not prove identical experience or one shared mind.

Machines can synchronize too.

== Shared belief is not necessarily true ==

A whole group can share one false belief.

In science, shared claims become more reliable when they are supported by public evidence, independent testing, and correction.

Intersubjectivity helps make knowledge public, but it does not replace evidence.

== Artificial agents ==

Two AI systems can share a database, use the same reference, or coordinate tasks.

This can imitate some functional parts of intersubjective coordination.

It does not prove that the systems have subjective experience.

